;; Fermat, Methodus ad disquirendam maximam et minimam, and De tangentibus
;; linearum curvarum. Latin quoted from Oeuvres de Fermat, ed. P. Tannery and
;; C. Henry, vol. I (Paris 1891), pp. 133-136 (archive.org
;; oeuvresdefermat01ferm, public domain); :claim is the series' own English
;; rendering, :latin the original, :french the translation by P. Tannery,
;; Oeuvres vol. III (Paris 1896), pp. 121-123 (corpus 10-fermat/text/
;; oeuvres-t3-1896-french-tr.txt), the OCR read against the page. :stage names what
;; alexandria.fermat.maxima-scenes draws.
{:fermat/rectangle
 {:source "Tannery and Henry 1891, vol. I, pp. 133-134"
  :rule "Statuatur quilibet quaestionis terminus esse A ... et, inventa maxima aut minima in terminis sub A, gradu aut gradibus, ut libet, involutis, ponatur rursus idem qui prius terminus esse A + E, iterumque inveniatur maxima aut minima in terminis sub A et E gradibus, ut libet, coefficientibus. Adaequentur, ut loquitur Diophantus, duo homogenea maximae aut minimae aequalia."
  :french "On adégalera, pour parler comme Diophante, les deux expressions de la quantité maxima ou minima, et on retranchera les termes communs de part et d'autre."
  :steps
  [{:stage :problem :why "the problem"
    :claim "Let the line AC be divided at E so that the rectangle AEC is greatest."
    :latin "Sit recta AC dividenda in E, ut rectangulum AEC sit maximum."
    :french "Soit à partager la droite AC en E, en sorte que AE × EC soit maximum."}
   {:stage :name-a :why "name the term A"
    :claim "Call AC B, and one part of it A; the other is B - A, and the rectangle under the parts is B in A - A squared, which must be found greatest."
    :latin "Recta AC dicatur B. Ponatur pars altera ipsius B esse A: ergo reliqua erit B - A, et rectangulum sub segmentis erit B in A - Aq., quod debet inveniri maximum."
    :french "Posons AC = b; soit a un des segments, l'autre sera b - a, et le produit dont on doit trouver le maximum : ba - a²."}
   {:stage :name-ae :why "again, with A + E"
    :claim "Now let one part be A + E; the other is B - A - E, and the rectangle is B in A - A squared + B in E - 2 A in E - E squared, which must be adequated to the first rectangle."
    :latin "Ponatur rursus pars altera ipsius B esse A + E: ergo reliqua erit B - A - E, et rectangulum sub segmentis erit B in A - Aq. + B in E - A in E bis - Eq., quod debet adaequari superiori rectangulo B in A - Aq."
    :french "Soit maintenant a + e le premier segment de b, le second sera b - a - e, et le produit des segments : ba - a² + be - 2ae - e². Il doit être adégalé au précédent : ba - a²."}
   {:stage :common :why "take away what is common"
    :claim "Taking away what is common, B in E is adequated to 2 A in E + E squared."
    :latin "Demptis communibus, B in E adaequabitur A in E bis + Eq."
    :french "Supprimant les termes communs : be ∼ 2ae + e²."}
   {:stage :divide :why "divide by E"
    :claim "And, all being divided by E, B is adequated to 2A + E."
    :latin "et, omnibus per E divisis, B adaequabitur A bis + E."
    :french "Divisant tous les termes : b ∼ 2a + e."}
   {:stage :strike :why "strike out E"
    :claim "Strike out E: B equals 2A. Therefore B is to be cut in half; nor can a more general method be given."
    :latin "Elidatur E, B aequabitur A bis. Igitur B bifariam est dividenda ad solutionem propositi; nec potest generalior dari methodus."
    :french "Supprimez e : b = 2a. Pour résoudre le problème il faut donc prendre la moitié de b. Il est impossible de donner une méthode plus générale."}
   {:stage :derivative :why "Leibniz 1684"
    :claim "What is left after E is struck out is what Leibniz will write d(BA - AA)/dA = B - 2A, and setting it to nothing is setting the derivative to zero."}]}

 :fermat/tangent
 {:source "Tannery and Henry 1891, vol. I, pp. 134-136"
  :steps
  [{:stage :parabola :why "the problem"
    :claim "Let the parabola BDN be given, vertex D, diameter DC, and B on it, at which a line BE is to be drawn touching the parabola and meeting the diameter at E."
    :latin "Sit data, verbi gratia, parabole BDN, cujus vertex D, diameter DC, et punctum in ea datum B, ad quod ducenda est recta BE tangens parabolen et in puncto E cum diametro concurrens."
    :french "Soit donnée, par exemple, la parabole BDN, de sommet D, de diamètre DC; soit donné sur elle le point B, par lequel il faut mener la droite BE tangente à la parabole et rencontrant le diamètre en E."}
   {:stage :outside :why "O lies outside the curve"
    :claim "Take any point O on BE and the ordinate OI; since O is outside the parabola, CD : DI is greater than BC squared : OI squared, that is, by similar triangles, than CE squared : IE squared."
    :french "Si l'on prend sur la droite BE un point quelconque O, dont on mène l'ordonnée OI, en même temps que l'ordonnée BC du point B, on aura CD : DI > BC² : OI², puisque le point O est extérieur à la parabole."}
   {:stage :name :why "name the lines"
    :claim "CD is given, call it D; let CE be A and CI be E. Then D : D - E has a greater ratio than A squared : A squared + E squared - 2 A E."
    :latin "sit igitur CD aequalis D datae. Ponatur CE esse A: ponatur CI esse E."
    :french "Soit donc CD = d, donnée. Posons CE = a et CI = e."}
   {:stage :adequate :why "adequate, as for a maximum"
    :claim "Multiplying, and adequating by the method above, with what is common taken away: D in E squared - 2 D in A in E is adequated to - A squared in E."
    :latin "Adaequentur igitur juxta superiorem methodum"
    :french "Adégalons donc, d'après la méthode précédente; on aura, en retranchant les termes communs : de² - 2dae ∼ - a²e."}
   {:stage :strike :why "divide by E, strike E"
    :claim "Divide all by E and strike out D in E: A squared equals 2 D in A, so A equals 2D."
    :french "Divisez tous les termes par e : de + a² ∼ 2da. Supprimez de : il reste a² = 2da, donc a = 2d."}
   {:stage :subtangent :why "the result"
    :claim "So we have proved CE double CD, which is indeed so. Nor does the method ever fail."
    :latin "Ergo CE probavimus duplam ipsius CD, quod quidem ita se habet. Nec unquam fallit methodus"
    :french "Nous prouvons ainsi que CE est double de CD, ce qui est conforme à la vérité. Cette méthode ne trompe jamais."}]}}
